WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.199
You can predict

00:00:01.010 --> 00:00:01.906
the LoRA size

00:00:01.718 --> 00:00:02.967
a dataset needs

00:00:03.486 --> 00:00:04.559
The authors built

00:00:04.382 --> 00:00:05.502
a predictive theory

00:00:05.314 --> 00:00:05.997
of how much

00:00:05.809 --> 00:00:06.941
a LoRA adapter

00:00:06.752 --> 00:00:07.660
can hold as

00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:08.285
a function of

00:00:08.097 --> 00:00:08.851
its rank

00:00:09.382 --> 00:00:10.549
Given a fine-tuning

00:00:10.361 --> 00:00:11.834
dataset the theory

00:00:11.646 --> 00:00:12.801
predicts the size

00:00:12.613 --> 00:00:13.556
required to fit

00:00:13.368 --> 00:00:13.756
it

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:15.183
landing within zero

00:00:14.995 --> 00:00:16.268
point four percent

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:16.846
of the exact

00:00:16.658 --> 00:00:18.084
capacity at five

00:00:17.896 --> 00:00:19.087
hundred twelve by

00:00:18.898 --> 00:00:20.490
one thousand twenty-four

00:00:21.014 --> 00:00:22.452
Smaller adapters are

00:00:22.264 --> 00:00:23.289
lighter to load

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.634
faster to generate

00:00:24.446 --> 00:00:25.223
text from

00:00:25.354 --> 00:00:25.707
and

00:00:25.743 --> 00:00:26.792
easier to store

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:27.782
and rank one

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:28.608
can already hold

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:29.103
a lot

00:00:29.622 --> 00:00:30.636
Full story on

00:00:30.447 --> 00:00:31.343
the dashboard
